WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.880
One of the great challenges in medicine,
especially in the modern era is

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:07.280
the healthcare leader,
is trying to balance big data,

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:12.550
cutting edge research with a fundamental
sense of caring for the patient.

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:15.242
How do you deliver the sense of care and

00:00:15.242 --> 00:00:19.127
caring while still delivering
cutting edge science?

00:00:19.127 --> 00:00:22.116
[MUSIC]

00:00:22.116 --> 00:00:26.517
&gt;&gt; I knew that anything that involved
Stanford GSB, the design school,

00:00:26.517 --> 00:00:31.366
was going to be something that was really
going to challenge your mind in a new way,

00:00:31.366 --> 00:00:33.880
not your typical leadership program.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:37.950
And then when I saw that it had the
interdisciplinary work with the medical

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:43.320
school, I'd felt like it was my
life wrapped up in a program.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:46.970
&gt;&gt; The Innovative Healthcare
Leader Program is a new offering

00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:50.750
here at Stanford University that's offered
jointly between the School of Medicine,

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:52.830
and the Graduate School of Business.

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:55.930
&gt;&gt; The Innovative Healthcare Leader
is tremendously exciting for me,

00:00:55.930 --> 00:01:00.480
because we're really capturing a synergy
that is everything that Stanford's about.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:04.130
&gt;&gt; Abraham said something in one of
his lectures that has stuck with me,

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:08.040
which was that the Stone Age didn't
end because we ran out of stones.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:11.530
And as I am thinking about things changing
in medicine it's not that the things we

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:15.020
have aren't working,
it's just new things keep coming.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:18.790
&gt;&gt; One of the goals of
the 21st century leader is to

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:23.190
remove the impediments to creativity,
remove the impediments to innovation.

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:26.050
And this course is really
about understanding

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:29.710
what elements in your culture or
perhaps even in your own personality or

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:33.240
in your workplace are actually preventing
you from allowing innovation to happen.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:36.370
&gt;&gt; One of the great strengths
of the Graduate School of

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:39.860
Business is the way that they've
championed design thinking.

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:40.752
&gt;&gt; The D school's unique.

00:01:40.752 --> 00:01:43.490
That was one of the wonderful
parts of this program.

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:46.070
It helped strip us down a little bit.

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:49.830
The team approach, coming together
with people that you didn't know and

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:53.500
through the process of working together
here we challenged each other and

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.870
came up with different ways
of thinking of a problem.

00:01:55.870 --> 00:02:00.560
The design thinking methodology allows
us to break big problems down into

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:05.500
small manageable chunks, and
then innovate to develop solutions for

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:09.000
those problems in a way that
feels much less overwhelming.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:13.410
&gt;&gt; We're on the edge of
this destructive industry.

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:17.530
And it's really helpful to
step back from that a bit and

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:21.195
focus on strategies to be even
more effective in that role.

00:02:21.195 --> 00:02:26.655
&gt;&gt; I'm leading a program that has grown
dramatically over the past two years.

00:02:26.655 --> 00:02:30.615
I really hadn't thought about
some of the challenges that

00:02:30.615 --> 00:02:34.562
would come up in that process, and I feel
like I'm going back with some tools to

00:02:34.562 --> 00:02:39.662
better manage that change now and to help
my team better be a part of that process.

00:02:39.662 --> 00:02:43.162
&gt;&gt; A bit of compass resetting is
an important part of the week here.

00:02:43.162 --> 00:02:46.382
So at the same time that it does
represent an investment in time,

00:02:46.382 --> 00:02:50.492
the return on that investment is
the chance to rethink the direction that

00:02:50.492 --> 00:02:55.140
you're moving in in your institution or
in ones hospital or workplace.

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:59.010
&gt;&gt; The healthcare leader of the future
needs to worry about the patient, but

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:02.450
also about the welfare of
the people taking care of them.

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:05.508
Nothing else is going to work
if your workforce is unhappy.

00:03:05.508 --> 00:03:10.370
&gt;&gt; This intersection of some of the best
minds in medicine and some of the best

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:15.670
minds in business will offer healthcare
leaders tools for leadership and

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:20.150
innovation to manage change in this
ever-changing healthcare environment.

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:22.030
How do we keep that art in medicine?

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:26.230
How do we keep that patient contact
in spite of all this technology and

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:28.560
things that are being pulled from us?

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:34.750
&gt;&gt; I think there's a gap between
our intent and the actual execution

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:38.550
between the wonderful technology
we have and the fundamental sense.

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:42.738
As Peabody said many,
many years ago that the secret of the care

00:03:42.738 --> 00:03:45.673
of the patient is in caring for
the patient.

00:03:45.673 --> 00:03:50.249
[MUSIC]

